answer1:
The specific constitutional grounds for disqualification from being chosen as or being a member of either House of Parliament are as follows:

* Being under any acknowledgment of allegiance or adherence to a foreign State (Article 84(e));
* Holding an office of profit under the Government of India or the Government of any State (Article 102(1));
* Being disqualified under the Tenth Schedule (Article 102(2));
* Being a member of a House of the Legislature of a State if he is so disqualified under the Tenth Schedule (Article 191(2)).

answer2:
According to Article 102, the specific constitutional grounds for disqualification from being chosen as or being a member of either House of Parliament are:

(a) holding any office of profit under the Government of India or the Government of any State, other than an office declared by Parliament by law not to disqualify its holder;
(b) being of unsound mind and stands so declared by a competent court;
(c) being an undischarged insolvent;
(d) not being a citizen of India, or having voluntarily acquired the citizenship of a foreign State, or being under any acknowledgment of allegiance or adherence to a foreign State; and
(e) being so disqualified by or under any law made by Parliament.
